```ADD_PAPER
2412.07942v1
This paper develops a mathematical theory of neural scaling laws based on percolation theory. The authors model natural datasets as hypercubic lattices with context-dependent target functions, where adjacent data-space elements requiring interchangeable behavior are connected by bonds. At the percolation threshold, the cluster size distribution follows a power law, yielding a Zipf-like Q Sequence of discrete subtasks (quanta), thereby grounding the quantization model of Michaud et al.; above threshold, a single dominant cluster corresponds to the manifold approximation model of Sharma and Kaplan. The theory predicts power-law exponents for model scaling (α or c/D depending on regime) and data scaling (α/(1+α) or c/D depending on regime). Experiments used a minimal toy setting with percolation clusters simulated on a Bethe lattice (d=100, critical occupation probability) and nearest-neighbor regression augmented with a Bayesian prior. The loss was measured for model scaling and data scaling across 50 random seeds; theoretical predictions (with no free parameters after fixing cluster DOF scaling) matched empirical scaling curves well, with model scaling and data scaling both following predicted power laws. The paper also connects the derived data-scaling exponent α/(1+α)=0.5 for LLMs to Chinchilla results, suggesting possible headroom in data efficiency.
``` 
