```ADD_PAPER
2201.12150v2
This survey formalizes learning curves in supervised machine learning, distinguishing sample-wise curves (performance vs. training set size) and iteration-wise curves (performance vs. training iterations). It defines anchor points, saturation point, limit performance, and utility-based stopping points, and introduces a three-axis framework for categorizing learning-curve methods: decision-making situation (data acquisition, early stopping, early discarding), technical question asked (binary, ranking, value prediction, full curve modeling), and data resources used (target dataset, other datasets, dataset meta-features, learner features). The literature review covers both model-free approaches (e.g., progressive sampling, successive halving, hyperband, metalearning-based candidate ranking) and model-based approaches (inverse power law curve fitting, range/confidence-interval estimates, Bayesian/MCMC distribution estimates, Gaussian-process and Bayesian-neural-network portfolio models). The paper also surveys empirical learning-curve databases and notes the scarcity of direct quantitative comparisons between extrapolation methods, proposing future work on benchmarks and tighter integration with AutoML.
